// 0.0.6 std library: `collection.iter` — eager iterator helpers (F4).
//
// `map` / `filter` / `foldl` / `foldr` / `take` are schematic builtins: the
// compiler resolves the type variables per call site and expands each call
// **inline** (there is no runtime call, and — without traits — no iterator
// object). This module's header is the API contract; the real signatures
// live in `typeck/builtins.rs` (the 0.0.3 V06 schematic mechanism, extended
// to two type variables for `map`/`foldl`/`foldr`).
//
// **Breaking change (0.0.6, D5/D6)**: `fold` is renamed `foldl` — the old
// `fold` was a left fold, and keeping a direction-ambiguous name while
// adding `foldr` would fossilize the ambiguity. No alias is provided.
//
//     import std.collection.iter;
//
//     xs = [1, 2, 3, 4];
//     doubled = std.collection.iter.map(xs, twice);      // [2, 4, 6, 8]
//     evens   = std.collection.iter.filter(xs, is_even); // [2, 4]
//     total   = std.collection.iter.foldl(xs, 0, add);   // 10
//     total_r = std.collection.iter.foldr(xs, 0, sub);   // right fold
//     head    = std.collection.iter.take(xs, 2);         // [1, 2]
//
//   where `twice`, `is_even`, `add` and `sub` are ordinary `func`s.
//
// Fold directions (0.0.6 D5):
// - `foldl(xs, init, f)` calls `f(acc, elem)` left to right —
//   `foldl([1,2,3], 0, sub)` = `(0-1)-2-3`.
// - `foldr(xs, init, f)` calls `f(elem, acc)` right to left —
//   `foldr([1,2,3], 0, sub)` = `1-(2-(3-0))`. The array is walked
//   backward by index (no reverse copy).
//
// All helpers are **eager** — arrays in, arrays out (or, for the folds, a
// scalar out). Lazy iterators need user-facing generics and wait for 0.1.0.
//
// Ownership: iterating reads elements as **copies** (bounds-checked deep
// copies for owned element types, D3/D4), so the source array is never
// consumed and the function arguments never *receive* the originals — the
// `move`/`clone` rules of a normal call do not apply to the element flowing
// into `f`. The result array is a fresh value, built incrementally with
// `std.arr.push`.
//
// `zip` is intentionally NOT here: it needs a two-element pair, and Fleen
// has no tuples (the V07 review decision; revisit with tuples, 0.1.0).
